Swift possesses a [[mezzo-soprano]] [[Voice type|vocal range]],{{sfn|Fulford|2014|p=192}} but she mostly sings in her [[alto]] range.<ref name="Powers-2014" /><ref name="Lambert-2014" /><ref>{{Cite web |last=St. James |first=Emily |author-link=Emily St. James |date=April 21, 2021 |title=Taylor Swift's Songs Haven't Changed. But She Has. |url=https://www.vox.com/culture/22386488/taylor-swift-fearless-taylors-version-review |url-access=limited |access-date=July 24, 2025 |website=[[Vox (website)|Vox]] |archive-date=February 2, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230202201310/https://www.vox.com/culture/22386488/taylor-swift-fearless-taylors-version-review |url-status=live}}</ref> Her [[Pitch (music)|pitch]], [[dialect]], and [[Accent (sociolinguistics)|accent]] have undergone many changes throughout her career.<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Basu |first=Mohana |title=Speak Now, Y'all: Taylor Swift's Accent Really Has Changed |url=https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/taylor-swift-speech-pattern-changed-over-time-linguistics-study-shows/ |access-date=September 26, 2025 |magazine=[[Scientific American]] |archive-date=October 3, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251003123500/https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/taylor-swift-speech-pattern-changed-over-time-linguistics-study-shows/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Reviews of her early country albums criticized her vocals as weak and strained compared to those of other female country singers.{{sfn|Provenzano|2018|p=173}} Defenders of Swift appreciated that she refrained from correcting her pitch with [[Auto-Tune]] and that she prioritized intimacy and emotion to communicate the messages of her songs with her audience,{{Sfnm|1a1=Provenzano|1y=2018|1pp=173-174|2a1=Pecknold|2y=2016|2p=81}} a style that critics have described as conversational.<ref name="Powers-2014">{{cite web |url=https://www.npr.org/sections/therecord/2014/10/30/360129727/the-many-new-voices-of-taylor-swift |title=The Many New Voices of Taylor Swift |last=Powers |first=Ann |author-link=Ann Powers |date=October 30, 2014 |publisher=[[NPR]] |access-date=June 19, 2024 |archive-date=December 26, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211226104654/https://www.npr.org/sections/therecord/2014/10/30/360129727/the-many-new-voices-of-taylor-swift |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/entertainment-arts-41886475 |title=Why Does Taylor Swift Write So Many One-Note Melodies? |last=Savage |first=Mark |date=November 8, 2017 |work=[[BBC News]] |access-date=June 19, 2024 |archive-date=February 8, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220208092800/https://www.bbc.com/news/entertainment-arts-41886475 |url-status=live}}</ref> According to the critic [[Ann Powers]], this singing style is demonstrated through Swift's attention to detail, which conveys an exact feeling—"the subtle adjustment of words and phrases to suggest moods like doubt, hope, and intimacy".<ref name="Powers-2014" /><ref>{{cite news |url=http://latimesblogs.latimes.com/music_blog/2010/10/album-review-taylor-swifts-speak-now.html |title=Album Review: Taylor Swift's ''Speak Now'' |last=Powers |first=Ann |author-link=Ann Powers |date=October 25, 2010 |newspaper=[[Los Angeles Times]] |access-date=October 25, 2010 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20101028003032/http://latimesblogs.latimes.com/music_blog/2010/10/album-review-taylor-swifts-speak-now.html |archive-date=October 28, 2010}}</ref>

On ''Red'' and ''1989'', Swift's vocals are processed with electronic effects, such as synthesizer tweaking, looping, and multitracking, to accompany the pop production.{{sfn|Perone|2017|pp=46–48}}<ref name="Powers-2014" /> Her voice on ''Reputation'' and ''Midnights'' incorporates hip-hop and R&B influences, resulting in a near-[[rap]] delivery that emphasizes rhythm and [[cadence]] over melody.<ref name="Powers-2022" /><ref>{{cite web |last=Cox |first=Jamieson |date=November 13, 2017 |title=Taylor Swift: ''Reputation'' |url=https://pitchfork.com/reviews/albums/taylor-swift-reputation/ |access-date=April 8, 2023 |website=[[Pitchfork (website)|Pitchfork]] |archive-date=November 13, 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171113115407/https://pitchfork.com/reviews/albums/taylor-swift-reputation/ |url-status=live}}</ref> She uses her lower [[register (music)|register]] vocals extensively in ''Folklore''<ref>{{cite magazine |url=https://www.billboard.com/lists/taylor-swift-songwriting-production-analyzed/ |title=Taylor Swift's Songwriting and Production Analyzed: 13 Secrets to Her Chart Success |last1=Penn |first1=David |last2=Trust |first2=Gary |magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]] |date=April 30, 2024 |access-date=June 19, 2024 |archive-date=April 18, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240418175946/https://www.billboard.com/lists/taylor-swift-songwriting-production-analyzed/ |url-status=live}}</ref> and both her lower and upper registers in ''Evermore''; the [[musicologist]] Alyssa Barca described her [[timbre]] in the upper register as "breathy and bright" and the lower register as "full and dark".<ref name="Barna-2020">{{cite news |last=Barna |first=Alyssa |date=December 16, 2020 |title=These Are the Musicological Reasons Taylor Swift's New Album Sounds Dull |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/outlook/2020/12/16/taylor-swift-musicology-evermore-boring/ |url-access=limited |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210225171621/https://www.washingtonpost.com/outlook/2020/12/16/taylor-swift-musicology-evermore-boring/ |archive-date=February 25, 2021 |access-date=November 3, 2021 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]]}}</ref>

The reception of Swift's vocals has been more positive since the release of ''Folklore''. The critic [[Amanda Petrusich]] commented in 2023 that her singing had become richer, with greater clarity and a stronger tone, even in live performances.<ref name="Petrusich-2023">{{Cite magazine |last=Petrusich |first=Amanda |author-link=Amanda Petrusich |date=June 12, 2023 |title=The Startling Intimacy of Taylor Swift's Eras Tour |url=https://www.newyorker.com/magazine/2023/06/19/taylor-swift-eras-tour-review |url-access=limited |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230804061915/https://www.newyorker.com/magazine/2023/06/19/taylor-swift-eras-tour-review |archive-date=August 4, 2023 |access-date=June 12, 2023 |magazine=[[The New Yorker]]}}</ref> ''Rolling Stone'' ranked her 102nd on their 2023 list "[[Rolling Stone's 200 Greatest Singers of All Time|200 Greatest Singers of All Time]]"; the magazine argued that her breathy timbre allows for a broad range of delivery and commented: "A decade ago, including her on this list would have been a controversial move, but recent releases like ''Folklore'', ''Evermore'', and ''Midnights'' officially settled the argument."<ref>{{Cite magazine |date=January 1, 2023 |title=The 200 Greatest Singers of All Time |url=https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-lists/best-singers-all-time-1234642307/taylor-swift-57-1234643043/ |url-access=limited |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230101171801/https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-lists/best-singers-all-time-1234642307/taylor-swift-57-1234643043/ |archive-date=January 1, 2023 |access-date=January 1, 2023 |magazine=[[Rolling Stone]]}}</ref> For Powers, Swift's versatile vocals are a result of her evolving artistry, combining "subtle interpolations of hip-hop's cadences and country crooners' relaxed timbre".<ref name="Powers-2022" />

=== Songwriting ===
Swift's fascination with songwriting began in her childhood; she credited her mother with igniting her early interest by helping her prepare for class presentations,<ref>{{cite news |last=Bream |first=Jon |date=December 7, 2007 |title=Music: OMG! Taylor's senior year |url=http://www.startribune.com/omg-taylor-s-senior-year/12149446/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170807021110/http://www.startribune.com/omg-taylor-s-senior-year/12149446/ |archive-date=August 7, 2017 |access-date=July 10, 2024 |newspaper=[[Star Tribune]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last=Newman |first=Melinda |date=December 19, 2008 |title=Taylor Swift Sessions Interview |url=http://music.aol.com/sessions/taylor-swift-change-sessions/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121009010116/http://music.aol.com/sessions/taylor-swift-change-sessions/ |archive-date=October 9, 2012 |access-date=March 25, 2011 |agency=[[AOL]]}}</ref> and would make up lyrics to [[Disney]] soundtrack songs once she had run out of words while singing them.<ref>{{cite news |last=Cairns |first=Dan |date=March 5, 2009 |title=Swift Rise of the Anti-Diva |url=http://www.theaustralian.com.au/arts/swift-rise-of-the-anti-diva/story-e6frg8n6-1111119032411 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141224030802/http://www.theaustralian.com.au/arts/swift-rise-of-the-anti-diva/story-e6frg8n6-1111119032411 |archive-date=December 24, 2014 |access-date=July 2, 2012 |newspaper=[[The Australian]]}}</ref> In her early career, her influences were the country musicians [[Patsy Cline]], [[Loretta Lynn]], [[Tammy Wynette]], and Dolly Parton,<ref name="Rolling Stone Interview" /><ref name="Roland-2011" /> and the 1990s female singer-songwriters [[Melissa Etheridge]], [[Sarah McLachlan]], and [[Alanis Morissette]].<ref>{{cite web |last=Bonaguro |first=Alison |date=November 8, 2012 |title=Offstage: Taylor Swift Inspired by Female Singer-Songwriters of the '90s |url=https://www.cmt.com/news/ceclr1/offstage-taylor-swift-inspired-by-female-singer-songwriters-of-the-90s |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231004015739/https://www.cmt.com/news/ceclr1/offstage-taylor-swift-inspired-by-female-singer-songwriters-of-the-90s |archive-date=October 4, 2023 |access-date=September 29, 2023 |publisher=[[CMT (American TV channel)|CMT]]}}</ref> In later interviews, she listed [[Joni Mitchell]] and [[Pete Wentz]] of [[Fall Out Boy]] as additional influences,<ref>{{cite magazine |date=June 22, 2016 |title=Joni Mitchell: 15 Great Artists Influenced by the ''Blue'' Singer |url=https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-lists/joni-mitchell-15-great-artists-influenced-by-the-blue-singer-168972/taylor-swift-30-154837/ |url-access=limited |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211226104717/https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-lists/joni-mitchell-15-great-artists-influenced-by-the-blue-singer-168972/taylor-swift-30-154837 |archive-date=December 26, 2021 |access-date=December 26, 2020 |magazine=[[Rolling Stone]]}}</ref><ref name="Hiatt-2019">{{Cite magazine |last=Hiatt |first=Brian |date=June 18, 2019 |title=Taylor Swift: The ''Rolling Stone'' Interview |url=https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-features/taylor-swift-rolling-stone-interview-880794/ |url-access=limited |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190918150500/https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-features/taylor-swift-rolling-stone-interview-880794/ |archive-date=September 18, 2019 |access-date=April 14, 2022 |magazine=[[Rolling Stone]]}}</ref> and [[Paul McCartney]], [[Bruce Springsteen]], [[Emmylou Harris]], and [[Kris Kristofferson]] as career role models for their consistent and evolving songwriting.<ref name="Widdicombe-2011" /><ref>{{cite news |last=Mansfield |first=Brian |author-link=Brian Mansfield |date=October 23, 2010 |title=Taylor Swift learns to ''Speak Now'', Reveal Her Maturity |url=http://usatoday30.usatoday.com/life/music/news/2010-10-22-taylorswift22_CV_N.htm |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121104201421/http://usatoday30.usatoday.com/life/music/news/2010-10-22-taylorswift22_CV_N.htm |archive-date=November 4, 2012 |access-date=July 1, 2012 |newspaper=[[USA Today]]}}</ref>

Swift considers herself a songwriter first and foremost.<ref name="Widdicombe-2011" />{{Sfn|Sanneh|2021|p=71}} She divides her lyrics into three types: "[[quill]] lyrics", songs rooted in antiquated poeticism; "[[fountain pen]] lyrics", based on modern and vivid storylines; and "glitter [[gel pen]] lyrics", which are lively and frivolous.<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Shutler |first=Ali |date=October 9, 2022 |title=Taylor Swift Organises Her Lyrics Into Three 'Dorky' Pen-Themed Categories |url=https://www.nme.com/news/music/taylor-swift-organises-her-lyrics-into-three-dorky-pen-themed-categories-3325355 |access-date=October 18, 2022 |magazine=[[NME]] |archive-date=October 18, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221018065417/https://www.nme.com/news/music/taylor-swift-organises-her-lyrics-into-three-dorky-pen-themed-categories-3325355 |url-status=live}}</ref> Using songwriting to cope with personal experiences, her songs are largely autobiographical and feature narratives that mostly revolve around love and romantic relationships.<ref name="Jo Sales-2013" /><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/SB10001424052702304510704575562401576663866 |title=Taylor Swift's Solo Act |last=Farley |first=Christopher John |date=October 22, 2010 |newspaper=[[The Wall Street Journal]] |access-date=May 24, 2012 |url-status=live |url-access=subscription |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150201211555/http://www.wsj.com/articles/SB10001424052702304510704575562401576663866 |archive-date=February 1, 2015}}</ref><ref name="Faichney-2024">{{Cite web |last=Faichney |first=Eloise |date=February 8, 2024 |title=''Fearless'': How Taylor Swift Is Owning Her Narrative |url=https://pursuit.unimelb.edu.au/articles/fearless-how-taylor-swift-is-owning-her-narrative |access-date=July 21, 2025 |website=Pursuit |publisher=[[University of Melbourne]] |archive-date=July 23, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250723095001/https://pursuit.unimelb.edu.au/articles/fearless-how-taylor-swift-is-owning-her-narrative |url-status=live}}</ref> She would start writing by identifying an emotion she wanted to convey, and the story and melody would follow.<ref>{{cite magazine |last=Gallo |first=Phil |date=October 22, 2012 |title=Taylor Swift's ''Red'': The Billboard Cover Story |url=https://www.billboard.com/articles/news/474541/taylor-swifts-red-the-billboard-cover-story |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130614085411/https://www.billboard.com/articles/news/474541/taylor-swifts-red-the-billboard-cover-story |archive-date=June 14, 2013 |access-date=July 21, 2025 |magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]]}}</ref> Where ''Taylor Swift'' and ''Fearless'' are rooted in adolescent feelings and detail an optimistic romance inspired by fairy tales, ''Speak Now'' reflects her young adulthood, offering newfound wisdom on real-life heartbreak.{{Sfn|Perone|2017|pp=21, 42}}<ref name="Caramanica-2010" /> ''Red'' explores the tumult of an intense breakup, and ''1989'' reflects on failed relationships with a wistful perspective; both albums incorporate lyrics that hint at sex, reflecting her personal growth.<ref name="Lambert-2014">{{Cite web |last=Lambert |first=Molly |author-link=Molly Lambert |date=October 27, 2014 |title=It's Hip to Be Swift |url=https://grantland.com/hollywood-prospectus/taylor-swift-1989/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241227170717/https://grantland.com/hollywood-prospectus/taylor-swift-1989/ |archive-date=December 27, 2024 |access-date=January 20, 2025 |website=[[Grantland]]}}</ref> Swift described ''Lover'' as a "love letter to love", inspired by her realization of "love that was very real".<ref>{{cite magazine |last=Aniftos |first=Rania |date=October 30, 2019 |title=Taylor Swift Compares ''Lover'' to ''Reputation'', Talks #MeToo Movement With Zane Lowe For Beats 1 Interview |url=https://www.billboard.com/articles/news/8541404/taylor-swift-zane-lowe-beats-1-interview |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200408215453/https://www.billboard.com/articles/news/8541404/taylor-swift-zane-lowe-beats-1-interview |archive-date=April 8, 2020 |access-date=November 14, 2019 |magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]]}}</ref>

As her career progressed, Swift wrote about self-perception and confrontation with her critics, influenced by fame, [[sexism]], and scrutiny of her personal life by the press.<ref>{{cite news |title=Taylor Swift's Songwriting: How the Star's Music Has Changed, For Better or Worse |url=https://www.cbc.ca/music/taylor-swift-s-songwriting-how-the-star-s-music-has-changed-for-better-or-worse-1.5246984 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211221212223/https://www.cbc.ca/music/taylor-swift-s-songwriting-how-the-star-s-music-has-changed-for-better-or-worse-1.5246984 |archive-date=December 21, 2021 |access-date=February 2, 2021 |publisher=[[CBC News]]}}</ref> This was first exhibited in ''Speak Now'', which set the precedent for the frantic media speculation on the subjects of Swift's songs, specifically concerning her dating history;<ref>{{cite web |last=Johnston |first=Maura |author-link=Maura Johnston |date=November 10, 2017 |title=''Speak Now'' Is a Peek Into Taylor Swift's Future |url=https://www.vulture.com/2017/11/revisiting-taylor-swifts-album-speak-now.html |url-access=limited |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171110174955/http://www.vulture.com/2017/11/revisiting-taylor-swifts-album-speak-now.html |archive-date=November 10, 2017 |access-date=November 10, 2017 |work=[[Vulture (website)|Vulture]]}}</ref> Swift considers this practice sexist.<ref name="Vanity Fair-2013">{{Cite magazine |date=March 5, 2013 |title=Cover Preview: Taylor Swift Fights Back About Her Love Life, the Hyannis Port House—and Has Words for Tina Fey and Amy Poehler |url=http://www.vanityfair.com/culture/2013/03/taylor-swift-fights-back-tina-fey-amy-poehler |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160808192844/http://www.vanityfair.com/culture/2013/03/taylor-swift-fights-back-tina-fey-amy-poehler |archive-date=August 8, 2016 |access-date=August 3, 2016 |magazine=[[Vanity Fair (magazine)|Vanity Fair]]}}</ref> ''Reputation'' both tackles the public controversies that tarnished her wholesome image and addresses a blossoming romance with intimacy and vulnerability; its extensive references to sex and alcohol set it apart from the youthful innocence that had informed Swift's past albums.<ref name="Powers-2019" /><ref>{{cite magazine |last1=Sheffield |first1=Rob |author-link=Rob Sheffield |date=November 9, 2017 |title=''Reputation'' Review: Taylor Swift Ditches Tabloid Drama on Most Intimate LP Yet |url=https://www.rollingstone.com/music/albumreviews/taylor-swift-reputation-sheffield-review-most-intimate-lp-w511359 |url-access=limited |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171110140315/http://www.rollingstone.com/music/albumreviews/taylor-swift-reputation-sheffield-review-most-intimate-lp-w511359 |archive-date=November 10, 2017 |access-date=November 10, 2017 |magazine=[[Rolling Stone]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Zoladz |first=Lindsay |date=March 9, 2021 |title=Taylor Swift and the Wisdom of Youth |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/09/arts/music/taylor-swift-folklore-youth.html |url-access=limited |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230105041314/https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/09/arts/music/taylor-swift-folklore-youth.html |archive-date=January 5, 2023 |access-date=January 5, 2023 |newspaper=[[The New York Times]]}}</ref> The nocturnal ruminations addressed in ''Midnights'' encompass regrets and fantasies, informed by Swift's self-awareness of her fame.<ref name="Light-2022" /> ''The Tortured Poets Department'' was conceived amidst her heightened fame in 2023, brought on by the Eras Tour and an intensely publicized love life.<ref name="Sisario-2024">{{Cite news |last=Sisario |first=Ben |author-link=Ben Sisario |date=April 19, 2024 |title=Taylor Swift's ''Tortured Poets'' Arrives With a Promotional Blitz |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2024/04/19/arts/music/taylor-swift-tortured-poets-department.html |url-access=limited |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240422193932/https://www.nytimes.com/2024/04/19/arts/music/taylor-swift-tortured-poets-department.html |archive-date=April 22, 2024 |access-date=April 19, 2024 |newspaper=[[The New York Times]]}}</ref> It explores heartbreak and other themes to extremes: erotic desires, forbidden love, and escaping from the public spotlight.<ref name="Powers-2024" /><ref>{{Cite news |last=Snapes |first=Laura |date=April 19, 2024 |title=Taylor Swift's New Album Is About a Reckless Kind of Freedom. If Only It Sounded As Uninhibited |url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/2024/apr/19/taylor-swift-the-tortured-poets-department-new-album-is-about-a-reckless-kind-of-freedom-if-only-it-sounded-as-uninhibited |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240607140512/https://www.theguardian.com/music/2024/apr/19/taylor-swift-the-tortured-poets-department-new-album-is-about-a-reckless-kind-of-freedom-if-only-it-sounded-as-uninhibited |archive-date=June 7, 2024 |access-date=May 9, 2024 |newspaper=[[The Guardian]]}}</ref>

On ''Folklore'' and ''Evermore'', Swift was inspired by [[escapism]] and [[romanticism]] to explore fictional narratives, deviating from the autobiographical songwriting that had characterized her artistry.<ref>{{cite magazine |last=Doyle |first=Patrick |date=November 13, 2020 |title=Musicians on Musicians: Paul McCartney and Taylor Swift |url=https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-features/paul-mccartney-taylor-swift-musicians-on-musicians-1089058/ |url-access=limited |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201130060712/https://www.rollingstone.com/music/music-features/paul-mccartney-taylor-swift-musicians-on-musicians-1089058/ |archive-date=November 30, 2020 |access-date=February 7, 2021 |magazine=[[Rolling Stone]]}}</ref> She imposed her emotions onto imagined characters and story arcs, inspired by authors and poets of [[Romantic literature|romantic]] and [[modernist literature]] like [[F. Scott Fitzgerald]], [[Robert Frost]], [[William Wordsworth]], and [[Emily Dickinson]];<ref>{{Cite news |last=Bate |first=Jonathan |author-link=Jonathan Bate |date=April 9, 2023 |title=Why Taylor Swift Is A Literary Giant—By a Shakespeare Professor |url=https://www.thetimes.com/article/why-taylor-swift-is-a-literary-giant-by-a-shakespeare-professor-3fmh269bt |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230618210707/https://www.thetimes.co.uk/article/why-taylor-swift-is-a-literary-giant-by-a-shakespeare-professor-3fmh269bt |archive-date=June 18, 2023 |access-date=March 14, 2024 |work=[[The Times]]}}</ref>{{Sfnm|1a1=Juko|1y=2024|1pp=237, 240|2a1=Smith|2y=2024|2p=78–80|3a1=Geary|3y=2024|3p=135}} the last of whom was a distant cousin of Swift.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Chasan |first=Aliza |date=March 4, 2024 |title=Ancestry Reveals Taylor Swift Is Related to American Poet Emily Dickinson |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/news/taylor-swift-emily-dickinson-distant-cousins/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240314013613/https://www.cbsnews.com/news/taylor-swift-emily-dickinson-distant-cousins/ |archive-date=March 14, 2024 |access-date=March 14, 2024 |publisher=[[CBS News]]}}</ref> The characters on ''Folklore'' and ''Evermore'' construct their narratives from fragments of memory, symbolizing the nature of folktales and oral traditions that endure over time.{{Sfnm|1a1=Hurley|1y=2024|1p=182|2a1=Juko|2y=2024|2p=240}}

Swift considers her songwriting "confessional",<ref name="Vanity Fair-2013" /><ref>{{cite magazine |last=Stubbs |first=Dan |date=October 9, 2015 |title=Taylor Swift: Power, Fame And The Future&nbsp;– The Full ''NME'' Cover Interview |url=https://www.nme.com/features/taylor-swift-power-fame-and-the-future-the-full-nme-cover-interview-549 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211221222630/https://www.nme.com/features/taylor-swift-power-fame-and-the-future-the-full-nme-cover-interview-549 |archive-date=December 21, 2021 |access-date=February 17, 2021 |magazine=[[NME]]}}</ref> and academics have linked her style to [[confessional poetry]], noting that her songs reference personal events and express her internal feelings to her audience.<ref name="Faichney-2024" /><ref>{{Cite web |last=Greenhaus |first=Rachel |date=September 23, 2015 |title=Taylor Swift: 1989's Confessional Poet |url=https://daily.jstor.org/taylor-swift-confessional-poet/ |access-date=July 22, 2025 |publisher=[[JSTOR]] |archive-date=April 22, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250422101520/https://daily.jstor.org/taylor-swift-confessional-poet/ |url-status=live}}</ref> Critical reception of her songwriting has been largely positive,<ref name="Widdicombe-2011" /><ref>{{Cite news |last=Empire |first=Kitty |author-link=Kitty Empire |date=June 2, 2024 |title='She Dominates Our Age': How Taylor Swift Became the Greatest Show on Earth |url=https://www.theguardian.com/music/article/2024/jun/02/taylor-swift-impact-records-eras-tour |access-date=June 2, 2024 |newspaper=[[The Observer]] |archive-date=June 13, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240613231547/https://www.theguardian.com/music/article/2024/jun/02/taylor-swift-impact-records-eras-tour |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="Lansky-2023" /> and her melodic compositions have been highlighted for optimizing the [[verse-chorus form]] with memorable [[Bridge (music)|bridges]].<ref name="Powers-2014" /><ref>{{cite magazine |last=Rosen |first=Jody |author-link=Jody Rosen |date=November 13, 2008 |title=''Fearless'' |url=https://www.rollingstone.com/music/albumreviews/fearless-20081113 |url-access=limited |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120815082027/http://www.rollingstone.com/music/albumreviews/fearless-20081113 |archive-date=August 15, 2012 |access-date=July 1, 2012 |magazine=[[Rolling Stone]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Bruner |first=Raisa |date=May 24, 2021 |title=How Olivia Rodrigo Become America's Biggest New Pop Star |url=https://time.com/6050587/olivia-rodrigo-sour/ |url-access=limited |access-date=November 10, 2021 |magazine=[[Time (magazine)|Time]] |archive-date=May 24, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210524205743/https://time.com/6050587/olivia-rodrigo-sour/ |url-status=live}}</ref> Several scholars have credited her with taking the confessional singer-songwriter tradition to new heights,<ref name="Sanders-2022">{{Cite web |last=Sanders |first=Sam |date=October 20, 2022 |title=Taylor Swift Is the Greatest Self-Portraitist of Our Time |url=https://www.vulture.com/article/taylor-swift-midnights-into-it.html |url-access=limited |access-date=July 22, 2025 |website=[[Vulture (website)|Vulture]] |archive-date=October 31, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221031090341/https://www.vulture.com/article/taylor-swift-midnights-into-it.html |url-status=live}}</ref>{{Sfn|Semler|2023|p=14}} and journalists have variedly described her as a "[[poet laureate]]"—of puberty,<ref name="Widdicombe-2011" /><ref>{{Cite web |date=May 19, 2013 |title=Taylor Swift: A Young Singer's Meteoric Rise |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/news/taylor-swift-a-young-singers-meteoric-rise-19-05-2013/ |access-date=July 23, 2025 |publisher=[[CBS News]] |archive-date=April 14, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210414001725/https://www.cbsnews.com/news/taylor-swift-a-young-singers-meteoric-rise-19-05-2013/ |url-status=live}}</ref> of romance,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Lambert |first=Molly |author-link=Molly Lambert |date=August 27, 2015 |title=Is This It? Has Taylor Swift Found True Love in Calvin Harris? |url=https://grantland.com/hollywood-prospectus/is-this-it-has-taylor-swift-found-true-love-in-calvin-harris/ |access-date=July 23, 2025 |website=[[Grantland]] |archive-date=March 13, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240313104807/https://grantland.com/hollywood-prospectus/is-this-it-has-taylor-swift-found-true-love-in-calvin-harris/ |url-status=live}}</ref> and of her generation.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Garfinkle-Crowell |first=Suzanne |date=June 17, 2023 |title=Taylor Swift Has Rocked My Psychiatric Practice |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2023/06/17/opinion/taylor-swift-mental-health.html |url-access=limited |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230619181023/https://www.nytimes.com/2023/06/17/opinion/taylor-swift-mental-health.html |archive-date=June 19, 2023 |access-date=June 17, 2023 |newspaper=[[The New York Times]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=June 21, 2023 |title=Why Taylor Swift Is the 'Poet Laureate' of Our Time |url=https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/audio/2023/jun/21/is-australia-in-its-taylor-swift-era-full-story-podcast |access-date=July 23, 2025 |work=[[The Guardian]]}}</ref> Some critics have dismissed her "confessional" style as material for [[Tabloid journalism|tabloid]] gossip.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Robson |first=David |date=April 22, 2024 |title=Sappho to Taylor Swift: The 2,600-Year-Old History of 'Tortured' Poets – And Sexism |url=https://www.bbc.com/culture/article/20240422-taylor-swift-tortured-poets-department-sappho |access-date=July 22, 2025 |publisher=[[BBC]] |archive-date=July 4, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250704175455/https://www.bbc.com/culture/article/20240422-taylor-swift-tortured-poets-department-sappho |url-status=live}}</ref> Objection to the perceived poetic value of her songs, mostly from rockist critics, views her as a pop star using literary subtexts as a commercial ploy, with metaphors that are at times imprecise or self-indulgent.{{Sfn|Hibbett|2022|pp=24–26}}

Scholars have attributed criticisms of Swift's songwriting to sexism. The musicologist Travis Stimeling argued that whereas Swift's autobiographical authenticity conforms to country and rock standards, her detractors, mostly male, view her lyrical depictions of a young woman's experiences as trivial and unworthy of serious merit.{{Sfn|Stimeling|2016|pp=90–91}} According to the English-language academic Ryan Hibbett, this gendered criticism bars Swift from receiving full artistic credentials, as does [[Bob Dylan]], whose reliance on romantic themes and occasional literary imprecisions are not as harshly criticized.{{Sfn|Hibbett|2022|p=25}} In the view of the literary critic [[Stephanie Burt]], although Swift's writing is not poetry in its traditional sense, it is proficient at "placing inventive, evocative language into pop melodies designed to be sung".<ref>{{Cite news |last=Burt |first=Stephanie |author-link=Stephanie Burt |date=July 1, 2024 |title=Taylor Swift, Poet |url=https://www.thenation.com/article/culture/taylor-swift-tortured-poets-department/ |url-access=limited |access-date=July 22, 2025 |work=[[The Nation]]}}</ref>

